NAME
       ssh-keygen - create and manage ssh key pairs and known host keys

SYNOPSIS
       ssh-keygen [-q] [-t TYPE] [-b BITS] [-N NEW-PASSPHRASE] [-C COMMENT]
                  [-f FILE]
       ssh-keygen -p [-P OLD] [-N NEW] [-f FILE]
       ssh-keygen -l [-E HASH] [-f FILE]
       ssh-keygen -y [-f FILE]
       ssh-keygen -R HOST

DESCRIPTION
       ssh-keygen creates key pairs for ssh logins. A key pair is two files:
       the private key (for example ~/.ssh/id_ed25519), which never leaves
       your account and must be readable only by you, and the public key (the
       same name with .pub), which you copy to the servers you want to log in
       to (see ssh-copy-id).

       Run without options, ssh-keygen asks where to save the key (Enter
       takes the default for the type) and for a passphrase. The passphrase
       encrypts the private key file; leaving it empty (press Enter twice)
       makes a key that logs in without any questions, which is what scripts
       and most exam tasks need.

       When the files already exist, ssh-keygen asks before overwriting them.

OPTIONS
       -t TYPE
              The kind of key: ed25519, ecdsa or rsa. RHEL 9 creates rsa keys
              when no type is given; ed25519 keys are short and fast.

       -b BITS
              The key size, for rsa (3072 by default) and ecdsa keys.

       -f FILE
              The private key file to create or work on. The public key is
              FILE.pub.

       -N PASSPHRASE
              The new passphrase. -N '' gives an empty one (no questions).

       -P PASSPHRASE
              The old passphrase, with -p.

       -C COMMENT
              A comment stored at the end of the public key, by default
              user@host.

       -q     Quiet: do not print the fingerprint and picture.

       -p     Change the passphrase of an existing private key.

       -l     Print the fingerprint of a key file.

       -E HASH
              The hash for fingerprints: sha256 (default) or md5.

       -y     Read a private key and print its public key.

       -R HOST
              Remove HOST's entries from ~/.ssh/known_hosts (after a server was
              reinstalled and has new host keys).

       -H     Hash the host names in a known_hosts file.

EXAMPLES
       Create an ed25519 key without a passphrase, without questions:

           ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

       Create a key interactively (Enter for the file, Enter twice for no
       passphrase):

           ssh-keygen

       Show the fingerprint of your public key:

           ssh-keygen -l -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

       Forget server2's old host key:

           ssh-keygen -R server2

       Add a passphrase to an existing key:

           ssh-keygen -p -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

FILES
       ~/.ssh/id_rsa, ~/.ssh/id_ecdsa, ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
              Private keys (mode 600).

       ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub, ~/.ssh/id_ecdsa.pub, ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
              Public keys (mode 644).

       /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key
              The server's own host keys, created when sshd first starts.

SEE ALSO
       ssh(1), ssh-copy-id(1), ssh-add(1), ssh-agent(1), sshd(8)
